From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 23:19:52 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(control): an injected character key is not ours to
 translate (KD-25)

The third cause, and the one the reporter was actually hitting. The two
previous fixes were both real defects and neither changed what he saw.

What the pane is handed while `A?:` is typed from the Windows App on
Android, from its own trace:

    key down vkey=0x10 scan=0x2A mods=0x0010   <- shift, a press of its own
    key up   vkey=0x10 scan=0x2A mods=0x0000   <- released before the letter
    key down vkey=0x41 scan=0x00 mods=0x0000   <- `A`, with no scan code

The client injects the character as a unicode event; Windows resolves it
to a virtual key because the character is producible on the current
layout, and hands the window a bare key with **no scan code** and no
modifier - the shift that would explain it having been released two
events earlier. So the layout is asked what key 0x41 produces, and
answers `a`. The same for `?` (arrives as VK_OEM_2) and `:` (VK_OEM_1).

Windows still composes the character the client meant for the character
message that follows, which is where a cascadia pane takes its text from
- and why a cascadia pane in the same window, on the identical events, is
correct. That contrast is what identified this: it is the measurement
that was missing all evening.

GhosttyKeyIsInjectedCharacter reports the key unhandled so the character
event delivers it, exactly as cascadia does. Scan code zero is the
signature - a key pressed on a keyboard always has one - so this is inert
for anyone typing on a physical keyboard.

Narrowed to keys that produce text. An injected arrow key, function key
or Enter has no character behind it and no character event will follow,
so those still go to the surface as keys and keep the engine's encoding.
The KD-24 exit keys are answered ahead of this and are unaffected.

Reproduced by posting the pair Windows really delivers - the scan-code-
less key, then the character message - measured with harness/rawin.
Before: `; : ? A a` typed gives `; ; / a a`, which is the reported bug
exactly. After: each character arrives.

unitControl 74/75 -> 75/75.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp    | 14 +++++++
 .../TerminalControl/GhosttyKeyTranslator.cpp  | 12 ++++++
 .../TerminalControl/GhosttyKeyTranslator.h    | 25 ++++++++++++
 .../UnitTests_Control/GhosttyKeyTests.cpp     | 40 +++++++++++++++++++
 4 files changed, 91 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
index 173081e0f..fcb89f8a0 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
@@ -970,6 +970,20 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
             return false;
         }
 
+        // An event with no scan code was injected rather than typed, and its
+        // virtual key cannot say which character was meant - see
+        // GhosttyKeyIsInjectedCharacter. Windows composes the right character
+        // for the message that follows, which is where a cascadia pane takes
+        // its text from and why it is correct on the identical event. Defer to
+        // it the same way: unhandled here, delivered by SendCharEvent.
+        //
+        // The modifier tracking above has already run, so a shift that arrives
+        // this way is still accounted for. This is only about the text.
+        if (GhosttyKeyIsInjectedCharacter(vkey, scanCode))
+        {
+            return false;
+        }
+
         // A VK_PACKET is a character the client injected, not a key: its scan
         // code is a UTF-16 code unit. Everything below that reads the scan code
         // as a scan code has to be told, or `A` is asked about as if it were
diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyKeyTranslator.cpp b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyKeyTranslator.cpp
index b40549086..c1e7db601 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyKeyTranslator.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyKeyTranslator.cpp
@@ -176,6 +176,18 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         return GhosttyKeyText(std::wstring_view{ &ch, 1 });
     }
 
+    bool GhosttyKeyIsInjectedCharacter(const uint16_t vkey, const uint16_t scanCode)
+    {
+        if (scanCode != 0)
+        {
+            return false;
+        }
+
+        // "Does this key produce text at all" - asked with no modifiers, since
+        // the modifiers are precisely what is missing from an injected event.
+        return !GhosttyKeyTextFor(0, vkey, 0).empty();
+    }
+
     // Deliberately keyed on the virtual key rather than on the text the layout
     // produces, which is what cascadia matches: its check lives in
     // SendCharEvent and compares the character against 0x04 and CR.
diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyKeyTranslator.h b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyKeyTranslator.h
index 42f869ec7..e0cc3820c 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyKeyTranslator.h
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyKeyTranslator.h
@@ -107,6 +107,31 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
     // second. Reset it when focus changes, like the modifier state.
     std::string GhosttyPacketText(uint16_t& pendingHighSurrogate, uint16_t scanCode);
 
+    // Whether this event was injected rather than typed, and carries a
+    // character that its virtual key cannot identify.
+    //
+    // A key pressed on a keyboard always has a scan code. Zero means the event
+    // was synthesized - and the Windows App on Android produces exactly that
+    // for every character: the on-screen shift is a press of its own that ends
+    // before the letter, and the letter then arrives as a bare virtual key with
+    // no scan code and no modifier held.
+    //
+    // Asking the layout what that key produces cannot answer the question.
+    // Virtual key 0x41 is the `A` key whether or not shift was meant, so the
+    // answer is `a` for a typed `A` - which is what the pane wrote, through two
+    // releases and two fixes. Windows itself knows, because it composes the
+    // character message from what the client actually sent, and that is where a
+    // cascadia pane takes its text from: which is why a cascadia pane is
+    // correct on the identical event.
+    //
+    // So a caller that gets true here should report the key **unhandled** and
+    // let the character event carry it, exactly as cascadia does.
+    //
+    // Only true for keys that produce text. An injected arrow key, function key
+    // or Enter has no character behind it and no character event will follow,
+    // so those are still keys and still belong to the engine. See KD-25.
+    bool GhosttyKeyIsInjectedCharacter(uint16_t vkey, uint16_t scanCode);
+
     // What a key press means once the child is gone.
     enum class GhosttyExitKey
     {
diff --git a/src/cascadia/UnitTests_Control/GhosttyKeyTests.cpp b/src/cascadia/UnitTests_Control/GhosttyKeyTests.cpp
index 09f746b26..92d70e72a 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/UnitTests_Control/GhosttyKeyTests.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/UnitTests_Control/GhosttyKeyTests.cpp
@@ -33,6 +33,7 @@ namespace ControlUnitTests
         TEST_METHOD(ShiftHeldOnlyInTheEventStreamStillShifts);
         TEST_METHOD(AModifierReleaseStopsApplying);
         TEST_METHOD(APacketCarriesItsCharacterInTheScanCode);
+        TEST_METHOD(AnInjectedCharacterKeyIsNotOursToTranslate);
         TEST_METHOD(TheKeysThatAnswerAnExitedProcess);
     };
 
@@ -253,6 +254,45 @@ namespace ControlUnitTests
         VERIFY_IS_TRUE(GhosttyPacketText(pending, 0x0D).empty());
     }
 
+    // The event the Windows App on Android really delivers, taken from the
+    // pane's own trace while the reporter typed `A?:`:
+    //
+    //     key down vkey=0x10 scan=0x2A mods=0x0010   <- shift, a press of its own
+    //     key up   vkey=0x10 scan=0x2A mods=0x0000   <- released before the letter
+    //     key down vkey=0x41 scan=0x00 mods=0x0000   <- `A`, with no scan code
+    //
+    // Scan code zero is the signature: a key pressed on a keyboard always has
+    // one. The virtual key alone cannot say whether `a` or `A` was meant, so
+    // the character has to come from the character event instead. KD-25.
+    void GhosttyKeyTests::AnInjectedCharacterKeyIsNotOursToTranslate()
+    {
+        const auto layout = GetKeyboardLayout(0);
+        if (LOWORD(reinterpret_cast<UINT_PTR>(layout)) != 0x0409)
+        {
+            Log::Result(WEX::Logging::TestResults::Skipped, L"not a US layout");
+            return;
+        }
+
+        // The three keys from that trace, all injected.
+        VERIFY_IS_TRUE(GhosttyKeyIsInjectedCharacter('A', 0));
+        VERIFY_IS_TRUE(GhosttyKeyIsInjectedCharacter(VK_OEM_2, 0)); // `/?`
+        VERIFY_IS_TRUE(GhosttyKeyIsInjectedCharacter(VK_OEM_1, 0)); // `;:`
+
+        // Typed on a keyboard, the same keys are ours to translate - this rule
+        // must be inert for everyone who is not typing through a remote client.
+        VERIFY_IS_FALSE(GhosttyKeyIsInjectedCharacter('A', 0x1E));
+        VERIFY_IS_FALSE(GhosttyKeyIsInjectedCharacter(VK_OEM_2, 0x35));
+
+        // Injected keys that carry no character stay with the engine, which
+        // owns their encoding. Handing these to the character event would drop
+        // them: no character event follows a key that produces no character.
+        VERIFY_IS_FALSE(GhosttyKeyIsInjectedCharacter(VK_LEFT, 0));
+        VERIFY_IS_FALSE(GhosttyKeyIsInjectedCharacter(VK_F5, 0));
+        VERIFY_IS_FALSE(GhosttyKeyIsInjectedCharacter(VK_RETURN, 0));
+        VERIFY_IS_FALSE(GhosttyKeyIsInjectedCharacter(VK_BACK, 0));
+        VERIFY_IS_FALSE(GhosttyKeyIsInjectedCharacter(VK_ESCAPE, 0));
+    }
+
     // The pane's process has exited and the connection has printed "You can now
     // close this terminal with Ctrl+D, or press Enter to restart". Which key
     // means which, and - as much as it matters - which keys mean neither.
